From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gigi <42325924+g-cqd@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:18:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Web Extensions: blocking webRequest.onBeforeRequest can
 cancel a request (Sunset W6a)

A Manifest V2 extension's onBeforeRequest listener added with ["blocking"] now decides before the request is sent:
{ cancel: true } fails the load (as a content blocker's block). Firefox and Chrome (Manifest V2) do this; uBlock Origin
filters with it. WebKit dropped "blocking" and ignored what a listener returned.

- The extension's process registers a blocking listener also as WebRequestOnBeforeRequestBlocking; the UI process tells
  every web process of the controller whether a context has one (SetHasBlockingWebRequestListeners, and in the context
  parameters for processes started later).
- A page whose extensions have blocking listeners marks its loads (pageHasBlockingWebRequestListeners); the network
  process holds such a load before it starts and asks the UI process (ResourceLoadWillSendRequest), which asks each
  context's processes with blocking listeners. A synchronous load is never held.
- The answers are gathered by a WebExtensionWebRequestDecision: answered at the last expected answer, or after two
  seconds whatever an extension does, under one shared timer (not one per request). The request's details are built
  only when a blocking listener matches.
- A blocking listener is not called again by the after-the-fact event.

Measured (release, 300 sequential requests, 10 runs): with a blocking listener that cancels nothing 297 ms, with an
observing one 267 ms: about 0.1 ms a request, only on pages whose extensions have blocking listeners. Pages without
them take no new step.

Not yet: redirectUrl, requestHeaders and responseHeaders changes (W6b, W6c).

